2475:**TWO TIERS OF GATE — "green gate" was ambiguous and cost a day.** 24 commits shipped on
2476:2026-08-13 with the full battery never run once; a red battery could not have been attributed
2477:to any of them.
2478:* every commit: its own TARGETED gate (~1 min), in the same commit as the fix;
2479:* WEEKLY (owner directive 2026-08-15: small unit tests any time, the full battery holds
2480:  off to weekly -- **SUNDAYS 18:00**, scheduled task `GXemul-weekly-battery`; it was
2481:  moved there from Saturday 03:00 and this line said Saturday until 2026-08-26.
2482:  *`nightly_check.sh:45-52` had ALREADY RECORDED that a doc "still said ... Saturdays
2483:  03:00 afterwards" -- the tool noticed the drift and the carrier kept it, which is the
2484:  structure-knew-and-prose-did-not shape again*), alone,
2485:  nothing else running: the FULL battery (~45 min, and `gate_ab` carries a load-sensitive
2486:  wall-clock oracle, so it genuinely needs the machine to itself).
2487:
2488:**FIX-TO-TEST RATIO: ship the detector WITH the fix, then stop.** ~1:1 in commits, ~30-40% of
2489:effort — not the 100% that 2026-08-13 spent. More verification is NOT what prevents mistakes;
2490:today was ~100% harness work and produced more errors than a normal fixing round. What caught
2491:them was EXECUTION and ADVERSARIAL attempts, not additional checks: three rounds of tightening
2492:a regex left it evadable, and one seat asked to break it found two evasions in a single pass.
2493:**For each fix, ask one quota-free measure-seat: "what is the smallest edit that breaks this
5:record and the derived-brief mechanism in a single session. An outage would have lost all of it.
6:
7:## What is here
8:
9:| file | what it does |
10:|---|---|
11:| `precommit_check.sh` | the pre-commit gate. Sections A–R. The later ones are not redundant with each other and each was built after a specific failure: **G** verifies a seat that ANSWERED was recorded; **H** verifies the seat was FIRED at all; **I** verifies that work which is OWED is written down (H reads a HELD marker as "waiting", and waiting is indistinguishable from forgotten); **I2** verifies the queue's own SUMMARY of those markers is current (it said "nineteen held stages" when the ledger held **thirty-four**); **J** hard-fails on carrier-copy divergence; **K** enforces the witness ladder; **L** asks whether the ledger noticed the code that shipped; **O** refuses one note stamped across several seat cells as independent review; **P** keeps the ledger and OUTSTANDING_BUGS in sync; **Q** fails on a stale published matrix; **R** verifies every committed detector is actually RUN by a gate |
12:| `install_hooks.sh` | installs `precommit_check.sh` as a REAL git pre-commit hook. **Built after the gate was bypassed twice in one day, by two mechanisms that both looked careful:** `precommit_check.sh \| tail -3 && git commit` (a pipeline's exit status is the LAST command's) and `... \| grep -E "FAIL" && git commit` (grep SUCCEEDS when it FINDS failures, so the `&&` fired precisely because the gate was red). An advisory check is a check that eventually is not respected. `git commit --no-verify` remains the escape hatch, deliberately loud. |
13:| `nightly_battery.sh` | runs the full 16-gate battery unattended and writes a stamped verdict. Invoked by the Windows scheduled task `GXemul-weekly-battery`, Sundays 18:00. |
14:| `nightly_check.sh` | the dead-man switch. Every threshold in the battery lives *inside* the run, so none of them can observe the run not happening. This asks the one question the battery cannot ask about itself. |
15:| `panel.sh` | convenes the seven scriptable review seats concurrently, verifies each produced a real answer, and persists that verdict to `$OUT/SEATS.txt` with a `PANEL_COMPLETE` sentinel. |
16:| `adjudicate.sh` | fires the three packet-fed seats at ONE seat's finding and asks them to refute it. |
17:| `panel_ollama.py` | the packet-fed seat helper. |
18:| `pipeline/ledger.json` | **the source of truth** for the review record. 74 rows × 9 seats × 4 phases. |
